#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# nest_while

#% text
# `nest_while(f, x, test)` applies `f` to `x`, then to the result, and so on, as long as `test` of the latest value is `true`; it gives the first value for which the test fails.

#% text
# `nest_while(f, x, test, 1, max)` applies `f` at most `max` times.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The test is applied to `x` first: if it fails, the result is `x` itself.

#% item
# Each step polls the interrupt, so an endless loop can be stopped; `max` bounds it in advance.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
nest_while(n => n/2, 1024, n => is_even(n))
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
nest_while(n => n + 1, 1, n => not is_prime(n + 1))
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# At most five steps:

#% cell
nest_while(x => 2x, 1, x => true, 1, 5)
#% output
#= 32

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The last element of `nest_while_list`:

#% cell
nest_while(n => n/2, 96, is_even) == last(nest_while_list(n => n/2, 96, is_even))
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `nest_while_list`, `nest`, `fix_point`, `fixed_point_list`
